there's a big trend in investing  you can see it on this chart of inflows  to esg funds  they peaked in 2020. esg funds say  they judge companies on environmental  social or corporate governance factors  and they aim to attract investors  looking for a more ethical approach  esg funds have been around for quite  some time especially in europe where the  trend has really been sort of ahead  of where we are in the us but it wasn't  until last year that we really saw an  explosion of interest  many of these funds have recently  outperformed the markets  but regulators say they may not be all  they claim to be  we'll explain why  [Music]  the tension around esg funds has to do  with the way that they're built  there are a few strategies including  exclusionary  single theme and best in class let's  start with the exclusionary approach so  when a  portfolio manager takes an exclusionary  approach to making an esg  fund will basically decide what category  of companies they don't want to have in  their portfolio no matter what  a really common category for this is  weapons manufacturers for instance or  tobacco companies or casino operators  those types of companies are commonly  totally excluded from  esg funds other esg funds tie themselves  to a single theme  the index ticker she tracks gender  diversity  by including large companies with at  least one woman in a key leadership role  that includes companies you might  recognize like paypal home depot and  netflix  there are also esg funds tied to solar  energy and water  which promotes sustainable use of  natural resources  funds like this can help an investor put  money into companies that might improve  society  esg funds can help an investor reach  their moral goals but the funds have  also earned  solid financial returns in recent years  a wall street journal analysis of esg  equity funds  found that nearly 150 of about 200 funds  outperformed their benchmarks that's  driven by tech stocks which are commonly  held by esg funds  more than half of these funds hold  microsoft nearly half  hold alphabet in other words esg funds  are betting big on tech  but critics have questions about tech  companies inclusion  tech stocks are sort of interesting  problem when it comes to esg because a  lot of these companies do have  relatively strong environmental  practices by 2030  microsoft will be carbon negative not  just across  our direct emissions but across our  supply chain too  but then on the other hand they may have  some serious problems when it comes to  the  s component of esg for instance we have  companies that have come under fire for  their data privacy  issues we have companies that have been  criticized for the way they treat  contractors and third-party workers  but here's the catch a fund manager can  just use looser criteria for which  companies it includes in the esg  this makes it easier for more companies  to qualify even ones that seem  counterintuitive to the overall mission  one such approach is called best in  class  when we asked blackrock last year how  they were putting together esg funds and  still including  major oil and gas producers they said  that they tend to take a best-in-class  approach  an oil company could still belong in the  esg fund if it's shown to have done  better on esg principles than its  competitors within the same industry  using a best-in-class approach means  that a company only needs to be better  on esg principles  than their competitors but an investor  doesn't always know  how that fund is built the problem is a  lot of investors don't tend to read the  fine print that comes with  all of their funds when they're deciding  what to put their money into  they may just be attracted to a fund  because they see the name of the ticker  or the name of the fund itself and think  that seems to align with my investing  goals but when you look  under the surface you'll often see there  are companies in these funds that don't  seem to align with the stated goal of  the fund and that really comes down to  the fact that there's still no standard  definition of esg  or what constitutes an esg fund  these details matter because they can  change how the fund performs  but critics say that they can also  compromise the fund's stated mission  for high ethical standards recently  critics in the federal government have  sounded the alarm  on sustainable investments here's sec  commissioner hester  pierce talking about how companies are  assessed for esg funds  we see labeling based on incomplete  information public shaming and shunning  wrapped in moral rhetoric  preached with cold-hearted  self-righteous oblivion to the  consequences  which ultimately fall on real people and  it's not just the sec  the labor department proposed a rule  this summer that would make it more  difficult for 401 k holders to put their  money in esgs  the department says the rule is needed  to protect workers from investments that  aren't in their best financial interest  but pension fund managers and democratic  lawmakers say  the labor department's proposal would  undermine a tool of positive social  change  regulators are still considering whether  or not to change any rules  some lawyers and regulators think that  there should be more standardization  across esgs to prevent investors from  being misled  and others say it's a step in the right  direction to have wall street thinking  about sustainability  at all
 

